.TH CP 1
.SH  NAME
cp \- copy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cp
file1 file2
.PP
.B cp
file ... directory
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I File1
is copied onto
.IR file2 .
The mode and owner of
.I file2
are preserved if it already
existed; the mode of the source file
is used otherwise.
.PP
.B Note:
`the mode of the source file is used' is not quite what happens, here or on the
PDP\-11.
.I Cp
hands
.IR creat (2)
the source's whole
.IR st_mode ,
and the kernel keeps
.B mode & 07777 & ~ISVTX
and then applies the caller's
.IR umask (2)
\- see
.B maknode
in kernel/sys2.c.  So the sticky bit is dropped and the umask still bites.
.B CMASK
is 0 on this system and nothing sets another, so today the copy does come out
with the source's permission bits; give the shell a
.I umask
builtin and it will not.
.PP
.B Note:
.I cp
does not preserve the times.  The copy is new, so its `accessed' and `updated'
times are the time of the copy.
.IR Mv (1)
restores them itself with
.IR utime (2)
after it has had to fall back on
.IR cp .
.PP
In the second form, one or more
.I files
are copied into the
.I directory
with their original file-names.
.PP
.B Note:
the constructed name \- the directory, a slash, and the last component of the
source \- is refused if it will not fit 512 characters.  v7 built it in the I/O
buffer with no bound at all.
.PP
.I Cp
refuses to copy a file onto itself.
.PP
.B Note:
.I cp
copies one 3072\-byte block at a time, which is
.B BUFSIZ
and is one filesystem block on this machine (include/sys/param.h).  v7's 512 was
one PDP\-11 disk block and would be six system calls per block here.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Cp
writes to the standard error and exits with the number of files it failed on.
.PP
.B cp: cannot open
.I file
\- the source could not be opened for reading.
.PP
.B cp: cannot create
.I file
\- the target could not be created: no write permission on its directory, or a
full filesystem.
.PP
.B cp:
.IB directory / file :
.B name too long
\- the constructed name exceeds the buffer; see above.
.PP
.B cp: cannot copy file to itself.
.PP
.B cp: read error
and
.B cp: write error.
\- the copy stopped part way, and the target is left truncated.
.SH BUGS
.I Cp
does not copy directories, and says nothing useful when asked to: it opens the
directory, reads it, and writes its raw contents into an ordinary file.  Use
.IR tar (1)
when there is one, or copy the files one at a time.
.PP
A copy interrupted part way leaves a truncated target.  Nothing here catches a
signal.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cat(1), pr(1), mv(1), ln(1), rm(1), creat(2), umask(2)
